tinytris (247 bytes), read from its disassembly

Source: `objdump -D -b binary -m i8086 -M intel --adjust-vma=0x100 tinytris.com` on the fetched .COM (its repo ships no
.asm). What is here is what I read in the bytes and then confirmed under the harness.

data layout
- The board is the stack: `mov bx,sp` (bx = 0xfffe). 24 rows of one word each, row i at bx + 2i modulo 64 KB, so the
  first rows wrap round to the PSP at 0x0000..0x002d. A 25th row of 0xffff is the floor.
- One row is 13 bits: template 0x1003 (bits 0-1 left wall, bit 12 right wall, bits 2-11 the ten cells). A full row is
  0x1fff.
- A piece is 16 bits, a 4x4 mask with one nibble per row. The seven shapes are a 14-byte table at the end of the file
  (2222 4460 2260 06c0 0c60 0660 04e0); there is no table of rotations, rotation is computed (46 bytes, a transpose by
  a shift/or loop).
- The piece comes from the timer counter (`in al,0x40`), `aam 7`, an index into the table.

tricks
1. A self-modifying routine: the opcode of `and [si],ax` is overwritten with 0x23 (`and ax,[si]`: test), 0x09 (`or
   [si],ax`: stamp) or 0x31 (`xor [si],ax`: toggle), so one loop does test, settle and draw.
2. The collision result is in ZF: the routine ORs every overlap into di, and `or di,di` leaves ZF = "no collision".
3. Undo by the stack: a move is applied and tested; the caller's `push cx / push dx` copies are discarded (`pop ax;
   pop ax`) or restored (`pop dx; pop cx`). `push 0x138` first makes `ret` jump back to the loop.
4. Row clear is a backward `movsw` loop under `std`, with no row counting.
5. It draws straight into text video memory: 13 cells of 2 characters per row, attribute 0x44 or 0x77, 24 rows.
6. Gravity is a free-running counter: `add ch,0x20; jae` drops the piece every 8th pass.

entry assumptions it makes
DS=CS=SS, a writable stack, text mode already on, and SI=0x100 (`cmp [si],0x1fff` reads the program's own first bytes
on the first pass).

behaviour under dosvm.py
- Keys: j left, l right, k rotate; every other key is read and ignored.
- A frame is one INT 16h AH=1 peek, then an AH=0 read only if a key is there. Gravity is every 8th frame; one frame is
  about 44,000 instructions, almost all of it redrawing.
- No pause: the archive's "pause" is the emulator's hotkey.
- Game over is a freeze: the spawn/land loop spins without calling the keyboard, so keys do nothing.
- The left wall is one cell wide and the right wall two, so the well is not centred.

defects found by the extra checks (not reported upstream)
Neither changes the claim: tinytris still passes the six feature checks.

1. A vertical I is lost at the right wall. The collision test shifts a nibble left by CL (`shl ax,cl`). Pressing `l`
   once too often takes CL to 0xff; the 80186 masks the count to 5 bits, 31 shifts a whole nibble out, and a cell that
   no longer exists can never collide. The piece disappears from the screen and from the board. `make holder-bug`
   shows it (fuzz.py). microtris moves the well up two bits so that CL = 0 is never a valid position, and reach.py
   checks that no reachable position loses a cell.
2. The draw loop does not end where it says. It is `mov cl,24 ... dec cx / jae .row`: `dec` leaves CF alone, so the loop
   ends only when `add di,0x6c` finally carries, after about 410 rows, when di wraps. Every frame it writes about 64 KB
   over ES=B800: the other seven text pages and, above them, 0xc0000..0xc7fff (video BIOS ROM area, where a real bus
   ignores the writes and an emulator may not). This is also most of its 44,000 instructions per frame, so it sets its
   gravity speed. `make holder-bug` shows it (footprint.py; the VM maps all 1 MB as RAM, which is why the six checks
   never notice). microtris draws 25 rows with `loop` at the same size and compensates in the gravity step (`add ch,2`,
   every 128th pass); `make speed` measures both.
